    feat(installer): first-class pack replace via --rule-pack-file --replace-rule-packs. Addresses TJ-DF-041.
    
    The pack edit-iterate loop previously required an undocumented manual
    uninstall+reinstall: --rule-pack-file refused any file whose derived name
    was already installed, with no in-place replacement path.
    
    --replace-rule-packs is a MODIFIER of --rule-pack-file, never a mode:
    
    - Without it, behavior is unchanged: the existing skip fires, now hinting
      at the new flag (the old --unrule-pack hint remains as the second
      remedy).
    - With it, the replacement follows the same fail-closed pipeline: parser
      charset gate -> prefix-scope skip -> file-exists -> python3/PyYAML
      preflight -> validator. Only AFTER the validator passes is the installed
      terminal-jail-pack-<name>.yaml backed up to <dest>.bak-<UTC ts> (the
      same .bak- family --uninstall sweeps) and overwritten. A refused
      replacement leaves the installed pack byte-identical and writes no
      backup (the backup site sits below the validator gate for exactly this
      reason — the first cut had it above the gate and the RED test caught
      the backup of a then-refused pack).
    - One UTC timestamp per run; the once-only guard is per-destination
      (.bak-<ts> existence), so a second file deriving the same name
      re-replaces silently without stacking a backup of the new copy.
    - Parse-time refusals (exit 2, nothing written): the flag alone without
      --rule-pack-file, in release mode, combined with --uninstall or
      --hermes-plugin; --replace-rule-packs=<value> is refused (bare flag).
    
    Usage text documents the flag; docs/quickstart.md gains "The pack
    edit-iterate loop" section. Tests: 6 new cases in plugin/test_install.py
    (skip pin, replace+one .bak, validator-refusal keeps pack byte-identical
    with no backup, flag-alone refusal, help+quickstart documentation, and
    the v2 replacement body fixture). RED-proved against HEAD before the fix
    (unknown-argument refusal on a throwaway worktree) and the full
    plugin/test_install.py suite run green on the final tree.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
